well blitek congratulations commissioner  first of all on the new job what does it  mean to you to be in charge now of the  London Fire Brigade just a feeling of it  immense pride you know I've been a  london firefighter for nearly 20 years I  love the job and in particular I love  this city the opportunity is given me  and to be able wear this uniform and  serve them protect the London public is  just I mean it's just a massive  privilege and to leave these fantastic  men and women at the brigade yeah I  couldn't be prouder but what a time to  take over at the start of a pandemic we  have obviously seen the ambulance  service right at the the forefront the  frontline the police trying desperately  to work out how to monitor the the  lockdown how is it impacted the fire  service I think ways demonstrate it is  as ever that we're a service that always  steps forward at a time when London  needs us I've got 300 firefighters  co-occuring ambulances with colleagues  from the London Ambulance Service I've  had over a hundred firefighters  providing services for those people who  lost their lives in their home so making  sure they're treated with dignity and  prepared for for burial or funeral  services I've had my logistics people  distributing their 10th million piece of  PPE on Friday so what what I think the  pandemic has done is allow us to step  into spaces that we perhaps wouldn't  normally occupy as well as delivering  our normal service to London and  Londoners you also took over quite soon  after a very critical  grenfell inquiry report phase one of the  inquiry what does that mean for the work  that you have to do for the for the fire  service what means is that we have to  bring forward the change required to  meet the recommendations laid down in  that report which we accept in full in  some of our systems in some of the  training we give them in some of the  leadership we provided them we let them  down and in letting them down we let  some of our communities down that night  and I'm very clear about that and  therefore I'm very focused on bringing  forward the change that's needed to  ensure that a tragedy like that never  happens in London again you were there  that night is it one of those events you  never really get over momentous  traumatic tragic for so many people for  me as tragic as it was it's been very  motivating you know  of family members who live on estates  just like that where the Tower of  Grenville was what it gave me was a  great sense of motivation to ensure that  it never happened again and to ensure  that you know the men and women of the  London Fire Brigade were never put in  that position again where they had to  respond to such a terrible tragedy what  was your personal experience personal  experience of it was that it was the  most serious incident I've ever attended  as a professional firefighter or officer  and I knew immediately on arrival  that my colleagues who were there and  who were risking their lives you know  time after time we were facing the  biggest professional firefighting  challenge of their lives and to be frank  what I saw in front of me was a  community torn apart a failure of the  building regulation system and and just  actually almost breathtaking the scale  of failure of the building and and how  we had so badly let so many people as a  society down in that community people  who should have gone to bed feeling  confident that they were safe where they  lived in modern London and yet had woken  up to a scene of utter carnage if you  had got there earlier do you think you  would have suggested that stay put was  revoked earlier I can only tell you what  my decision was in the moment and to me  in that moment it was very clear what  the appropriate decision was you know it  was no longer acceptable to advise  people that they were safe to stay put  and therefore it had to be changed  immediately so I think I can only answer  for my decision and I know that my  decision 